      <meta name="title" its:translate="yes">SUSE Linux Kdump Setup and Troubleshooting Guide</meta>
      <meta name="social-descr" its:translate="yes">Configure and troubleshoot Kdump on SUSE Linux</meta>
      <meta name="description" its:translate="yes">Learn how to install, configure and troubleshoot Kdump on standard and immutable SUSE Linux systems to capture memory snapshots during a kernel crash</meta>
      <abstract>
        <para>Kdump is a Linux kernel crash dumping mechanism in &productname; that captures a snapshot of physical memory (<filename>vmcore</filename>) when the kernel encounters a fatal error. By leveraging Kexec to instantly boot a secondary capture kernel in reserved RAM, Kdump bypasses corrupted primary kernel states. This enables administrators and developers to reliably perform root-cause analysis and debug silent system crashes without relying on failing logging daemons.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Prerequisites</emphasis></para>
  <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
      <para>Linux command-line fundamentals and administrative sudo privileges</para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>Adequate RAM reserved for the crash kernel via the crashkernel boot parameter</para>
    </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>
